# Doctor encounter scope, no doctor downloads, doctor-visible fields (rule 243)

Source: the internal memo of 2026-10-17 (M2.1, M2.2, M2.4, M3.1) and the owner's decisions of 2026-11-17 ("accept all
defaults"), recorded in `docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1.md` §2c. The design, impact analysis and PR split
are in `docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1-memo-implementation-plan.md`, section M2.1/M2.2 + M2.4 + M3.1.
Delivered in PRs; each PR adds its section. §2 (encounter scope, A1–A4) and §3 (doctor-visible fields, C1–C2) are
added by their own PRs.

## 0. Owner decisions (CG-22)

- M2.4 (2026-11-17): doctors must not download or print the legacy archive or the PTM (consent) print. The
  prescription print stays available to doctors. Front Office and Admin Klinik keep a legitimate print path.
- Plan default 4, accepted: the legacy odontogram source PDF is refused to doctors too; no watermark and no Android
  `FLAG_SECURE` in this wave.
- The encounter scope (§2) ships behind `rme.doctor_encounter_scope`, default OFF until a reviewed dry run, doctor UAT
  and an owner decision. §1 is a separate owner decision and is **not** behind that flag: it is live on merge.

## 1. No doctor downloads (PR "B — downloads")

1. **Owner revision.** This section revises rule 93 §12 (`93-legacy-rme-void-clinical-read.mdc`): print and export
   are no longer open to every reader of the archive — a Doctor is refused them. It extends the rule 138 §6 deny list
   (`138-doctor-room-scoped-access-and-print-deny.mdc`) and narrows rule 104's private-storage paragraph
   (`104-legacy-rme-doctor-workspace-embedded-pdf.mdc`): a doctor no longer receives the source PDF. Nothing else in
   those rules changes.
2. **One authority, one definition.** `App\Modules\RME\Services\DoctorRoomScopeService::deniesClinicalPrint()` decides
   every door below; it delegates to `DoctorPatientScopeService::shouldApplyDoctorScope()`: the Doctor role, unless
   the account also holds Owner, Super Admin or Supervisor RME. A Doctor who also holds Front Office, Admin Klinik,
   Kasir or an oversight role (Finance Klinik, Data Analyst) is still refused on every door below, including the
   staging and oversight doors that serve the same files. Never add a second "is this a doctor" predicate.
3. **The doors.** For a Doctor, each answers **403** with the rule 138 message
   (`DoctorRoomScopeService::CLINICAL_PRINT_DENIED_MESSAGE`) and writes one audit row through `AuditLogService`:

   | Route | Audit event | Payload `action` |
   |---|---|---|
   | `rme.legacy-records.source`, `.print`, `.export` | `DOCTOR_PRINT_LEGACY_RME_REJECTED` | `source`, `print`, `export` |
   | `settings.rme.legacy-imports.source` (staging, the same PDF) | `DOCTOR_PRINT_LEGACY_RME_REJECTED` | `staging_source` |
   | `oversight.legacy.rme.source`, `.print`, `.pdf` | `DOCTOR_PRINT_LEGACY_RME_REJECTED` | `oversight_source`, `oversight_print`, `oversight_pdf` |
   | `rme.legacy-odontograms.source` | `DOCTOR_PRINT_LEGACY_ODONTOGRAM_REJECTED` | `source` |
   | `oversight.legacy.odontogram.source`, `.print` | `DOCTOR_PRINT_LEGACY_ODONTOGRAM_REJECTED` | `oversight_source`, `oversight_print` |
   | `rme.consents.print` | `DOCTOR_PRINT_CONSENT_REJECTED` | `print` (plus `clinic_visit_id`) |

   Rule 138 §6 stays as it was: `rme.visits.print`, `rme.visits.pdf`, `rme.odontograms.print` are refused;
   `rme.prescriptions.print` is **allowed** to the doctor. Payloads are identifiers only: never a name, an RM number
   or a KTP/NIK.
4. **The layers, in this order.** The route permission stays. The record resolves through the caller's branch scope
   first, so an out-of-branch id is still **404** before any download rule. The reader's own `view` / `viewFile`
   check runs next, unchanged, and then the "only published bytes" check (`assertStreamable()`, the staging
   `assertBytesNotRetracted()`, the oversight resolver). A VOIDed archive therefore never reaches a download rule and
   never writes a doctor-print audit row; what it answers is unchanged: **404** on `rme.legacy-records.print`/`.export`,
   the staging source and the oversight doors, while on `rme.legacy-records.source` and `rme.legacy-odontograms.source`
   `viewFile` already requires a published record, so a non-Super-Admin reader (a doctor included) gets **403** there
   and Super Admin 404 (pre-existing). Then:
   - **Clinical, staging and consent doors (three layers):** the controller audits a doctor's real attempt (never the
     policy, which also gates `@can`), the policy refuses (`download` on `LegacyRmeRecordPolicy`,
     `LegacyOdontogramRecordPolicy` and `LegacyRmeImportPolicy`, `print` on `RmeVisitConsentPolicy`), and the
     controller asks `assertClinicalPrintAllowed()` once more, so a bypassed policy (the global `Gate::before`, a
     regression) still cannot serve a doctor.
   - **Oversight doors (no download/print policy; the per-record read policy and the `export` gate do not apply the print-deny):** after the route permission and the `clinic-oversight.export`
     gate, `OversightLegacyArchiveController` audits and calls `assertClinicalPrintAllowed()` itself — the controller
     and the service are the only doctor refusal there. A dedicated oversight ability is follow-up OD-MEMO-B-1.
5. **Doctors still read.** `show` and the page-image routes keep serving a doctor who may read the archive (inline
   PNG, `private, no-store`, `nosniff`); the consent page and its signature images stay readable. The workspace keeps
   the page images and the zoom/expand viewer of rule 104; an archive with no rendered page says so instead of
   falling back to the PDF. Honest limit: pixels on a screen can always be photographed.
6. **The UI mirrors the server.** Source, print and export controls render only under `@can('download', $record)`
   (the staging source under `@can('download', $import)`), the PTM print under `@can('print', $consent)`, the
   workspace source link only when `LegacyRmeRecordPolicy::downloadAny` allows it (it renders from a projection, not a
   model), and the oversight show screens' export controls only when the controller's `canExport` (export gate and
   not a Doctor) is true. When a page image fails and the reader has no source PDF, the workspace viewer says so for
   that page only (a per-page `pageFailed` flag, reset on open and on every page change) and stays in the image mode,
   so its page navigation keeps working. A hidden control is never the boundary.
7. **Everyone else is unchanged.** Front Office, Admin Klinik, Supervisor RME and Kasir (consent) keep their print
   path within their existing boundary (another branch's archive is 404, another branch's consent 403). Super Admin
   is exempt (and passes `Gate::before`). Owner holds no legacy or consent permission and stays refused at the route.
8. **Out of this section, and the recorded residual.** A pure Doctor holds neither the intake nor the oversight
   permission and is refused at the route on the staging and oversight doors; a dual-role Doctor is refused by item 3.
   The legacy odontogram staging screen has page images only (no source door). The 404 for a doctor outside the
   encounter scope (plan default 6) belongs to §2.
   **Residual (LOW, pre-existing, follow-up OD-MEMO-B-1):** a Doctor who also holds Finance Klinik or Data Analyst can
   still print the native RME and odontogram through `oversight.rme.visits.print`, `oversight.rme.visits.pdf` and
   `oversight.rme.visits.odontogram.print` — rule 138 §6's surface, never covered on the oversight screens and not
   changed here; and the oversight legacy-door refusal of item 4 is a single layer (controller + service). The
   follow-up is a dedicated oversight ability that applies the print-deny on both.
9. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorRoomScopedArchiveAndConsentPrintDenyTest.php` (critical-gate token
   `DoctorRoomScoped`, declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`).

## 2. Encounter scope (flag, PR A1)

1. **The switch.** `rme.doctor_encounter_scope` (config/feature_flags.php, risk `high`, default OFF, environment
   override captured at config build) is read only through `FeatureFlagService`, by
   `DoctorPatientScopeService::encounterScopeEnabled()` (CG-08; a dotted key breaks `config()` traversal). With it OFF
   every method of `DoctorPatientScopeService` answers exactly as before (Sprint 66.2: an active assignment row or any
   visit with the doctor grants the patient; refusals are 403; co-authoring an encounter in the same room, OD-5-1,
   stays). Turning it ON is a separate owner decision after every A-PR (A1–A4) is merged, a reviewed dry run of
   `rme:doctor-assignments-reconcile` (A4) and doctor UAT. The Android global enforcement is not touched.
2. **The window.** `App\Modules\RME\Services\DoctorEncounterWindow`: a visit is in service while `registered`,
   `waiting`, `in_progress` or `cashier_pending`, or `completed` with `completed_at` on TODAY's clinical date
   (`ClinicalClock`, Asia/Makassar; at 00:30 WITA the UTC date is still yesterday's and is never used). A cancelled,
   soft-deleted or undated completed visit is never in service. `isInService()` and its SQL twin
   `constrainInService()` are mirror images; change both or neither.
3. **One authority, read modes.** `DoctorPatientScopeService` stays the single authority; Owner, Super Admin and
   Supervisor RME stay exempt (`shouldApplyDoctorScope()`). With the flag ON, for a Doctor,
   `readModeFor($user, $patientId)` is `history` while the doctor has an own in-service encounter with the patient,
   `own` when they only have own visits, `none` otherwise. It is memoised for the current ROUTED request only, keyed
   by account and patient (rule 237 §12's pattern); jobs, commands and direct calls are never memoised. Assignment
   rows (manual, shared, auto_visit) grant nothing (plan default 1).
4. **Reads.** A visit is readable when it is the doctor's own (any date, never soft-deleted) or, in `history` mode,
   a NON-pre-exam visit of the patient (cashier_pending, completed, cancelled); its odontogram, prescription and
   consent follow it. A per-visit SHEET (`MedicalRecordPolicy::view`, and its handwriting images) follows its own
   visit the same way, so a doctor reads their own old sheet in `own` mode and never the sheet of another doctor's
   pre-exam visit. The patient's handwriting BOOK (`PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::canonicalMedicalRecord()`) and the
   patient as a whole (`doctorCanAccessPatient()`, patient-level policies, the patient-centric workspace in full) need
   `history`; item 11 (PR A3a) opens the workspace in `own` mode too, limited to the doctor's own sheets. Out-of-scope reads answer **404** (`Response::denyAsNotFound`, plan default 6; revises rule 99 §2 /
   AIR-LRM-25 for native records; the legacy archives follow in item 10, PR A2). An unlinked doctor account keeps its 403 "belum
   terhubung". Two refusals still answer before the scope and keep their 403, so existence stays visible there: the
   rule 138 room scope (an active visit of another room) and the prescription `documentLocked` check.
5. **Writes.** Only on the doctor's OWN encounter while it is in service: `doctorMayWriteVisit()` /
   `authorizeVisitWrite()` (403 "Kunjungan ini ditangani dokter lain…" for another doctor's readable visit, 403
   "sudah selesai dilayani" for the doctor's own visit out of service, 404 when not even readable). Policies:
   `ClinicVisitPolicy::update`, `transition`, `completeExamination` (and `startExamination`),
   `recordPerformedTreatments`; `OdontogramPolicy::author`, `update`, `finalize`; `RmePrescriptionPolicy::create`,
   `update` (asked only with the flag ON); `RmeVisitConsentPolicy::create`, `void`; `MedicalRecordPolicy::update` and
   `finalize` — a SHEET's typed fields, diagnoses (record, remove, make primary, the override) and finalisation follow
   the sheet's OWN visit, the book's typed fields included. The BOOK takes new handwriting pages through
   `MedicalRecordPolicy::appendPage` (patient-level `history`; `MedicalRecordHandwritingController` asks it for every
   book page) and the third layer then requires the live encounter to be the doctor's own. Third layer (re-asserted
   in the services, past `Gate::before`): `RmeVisitConsentService::actorMayWorkOnEncounter()` (RME book, odontogram,
   performed treatments) and `assertOperatorWithinScope()` (consent sign/void), and
   `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::permits()`; and for a SHEET's typed content (PR A1b)
   `MedicalRecordSheetWriteScope::assertWritable()` — the ANCHOR rule: every sheet, the book included, is typed-written
   only by the doctor of its own visit while in service — called after the RME authoring gate by `MedicalRecordService`
   (`updateDraft`, `finalize`, which take an explicit actor defaulting to the authenticated one; `finalized_by` records
   it), `MedicalRecordDiagnosisService` (`record`, `makePrimary`, `remove` — inside the transaction on the LOCKED sheet
   where it locks one), `DiagnosisRolloutService::grantOverride`, and the in-place page save of a per-visit sheet
   (`MedicalRecordHandwritingController`, inside the save transaction, after the authoring gate; lock order kept), with
   the same 403 messages (`AuthorizationException`). `startBook()` with typed content applies the same rule to the
   locked canonical visit it would anchor to (`assertTypedWriteOn()`), so typed content is never written onto another
   doctor's visit; a book started without typed content (the handwriting book) is allowed as before — chosen over
   silently dropping the typed fields. Appending a book page is the one patient-level write (`appendPage`).
   `MedicalRecordSheetWriteScope::followsItsVisit()` is the one definition of "not the book" for reads, shared with
   `MedicalRecordPolicy` (the book id is memoised per routed request once found). The workspace controls mirror these
   abilities, computed in `MedicalRecordController` (rule 135): the handwriting editor on `appendPage` for a book page
   and on `update` for an in-place sheet page, "+ Tambah Halaman RM" — which always writes a NEW page to the book,
   whatever page is shown — on `appendPage` of the book (`canAppendBookPage`, PR A1c; on a page of another doctor's
   sheet the in-place editor is withheld while the book still takes new pages), "+ Tambah Lembar RM" on `create` for
   its target visit, the diagnosis form on `update` of the sheet it writes. With the flag OFF all of these answer exactly as before (`appendPage` =
   the old `update`; the sheet assertions are no-ops).
6. **Lists: scoped now, and what waits for A3.** Scoped now and mirroring their per-record checks:
   `applyVisitScopeForUser()` = own visits OR (non-pre-exam AND an own in-service encounter with that patient) — the
   visit list, room worklist and patient queue; `applyPatientScopeForUser()` = patients with an own in-service
   encounter — patient search, cross-branch lookup, the unified medical-record index and the legacy lookups. The
   correlated subquery uses the window's SQL twin, never a re-derivation. The uncalled `applyMedicalRecordScopeForUser()` was
   removed rather than left as an unmirrored list scope. Histories (PR A1b): `ClinicVisitService::patientVisitHistory()`
   takes the viewer explicitly (rule 199's explicit-actor pattern; the visit page, the workspace and the prior-visit
   picker pass it) and, with the flag ON, keeps only the visits `doctorCanAccessVisit()` admits — so the visit page's
   "Riwayat Kunjungan Pasien", the workspace history and the legacy timeline built from them never list what the
   visit page would refuse. `doctorsWithAccessSummary()` shows a doctor viewer nothing with the flag ON (it describes
   the assignment scope that no longer grants anything and names other doctors). The prior-visit picker
   (`rme.visits.patient-options`) answers the scope's own status: 404 out of scope with the flag ON.
7. **Index.** `trx_clinic_visits (doctor_id, patient_id, status)` (`trx_clinic_visits_doctor_patient_status_index`,
   additive) serves the per-patient probe and the per-row EXISTS.
8. **Unchanged, and the preconditions for turning the flag ON.** BranchContext, `RmeWorkingBranchScope`, the rule 138
   room scope, the doctor session lock and the Android enforcement are unchanged. Room assignment is unchanged for
   Front Office and Admin Klinik; with the flag ON a Doctor places or moves a room (`assignRoom` authorises `update`)
   only on their own in-service visit; the doctor change and its revocation are A4. Not in A1: the legacy archive
   scope and its 404 (A2 — done, item 10), the after-service workspace for `own` mode (A3 — done in
   A3a, item 11), revocation and
   the reconcile command (A4), doctor-visible fields (C1/C2). The flag stays OFF until all of them are merged AND
   M3.3 is released. M3.3 is now in the base: re-saving an EXISTING book page that another encounter wrote (or a
   sealed one) is refused by rule 240 §4.4 in `MedicalRecordHandwritingService::save()`, with the flag ON and OFF
   (the test that pinned the gap now pins the refusal). The precondition recorded by the A3a review — the book's
   handwriting PAGES were read patient-level, so a page doctor B writes on the book during B's live encounter was
   listed to a doctor A in read mode `history` even when A may not read B's encounter — is closed by item 12: the
   book's pages follow their author stamp (`authored_clinic_visit_id`, `listsVisitFor()` on the authoring visit).
   Own mode still lists no book page (item 12, follow-up).
   Recorded by the A3 re-review, each to be closed in A4 before the flag is turned ON (flag-ON only, reads only, no
   write path): (a) at the anchor address `EnsureVisitRoomAssigned::gatedVisit()` gates the doctor's own roomless
   visit only when the request's `source_visit_id` names it — choose the gated visit on the server (the doctor's own
   live encounter, then an own in-service pre-exam visit of the patient) and use `source_visit_id` only to choose
   among the doctor's own visits; until then the room gate there is a redirect only and the RME authoring gate is
   what protects writes; (b) the workflow tabs (`visit-workflow-nav`) on the workspace show the UNREADABLE anchor's
   room state — render them for the source visit or omit them when `anchorContentReadable` is false; (c) `visit.room`
   runs before authorization, so another doctor's roomless pre-exam visit answers 302 where a placed one the doctor
   may not read answers 404 — authorize (404) before the room redirect for a scoped doctor.
   The items recorded open in A1 (service-level sheet re-assertion, history scoping, the access panel, the picker
   status) are closed by PR A1b.
   **The A3 list (recorded in A1c), each a precondition for turning the flag ON, and its status.** The per-record
   checks behind each already refused (404); only the listing could still name another doctor's visit.
   - The workspace sheet navigation and document rail (`PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::sheetsForPatient`, the native
     side of `RmeWorkspaceDocumentPresenter`) — **done, A3a** (item 11).
   - The book page sequence (`PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::orderedHandwritingBookForPatient`; the book is
     patient-level, the pages of per-visit sheets the doctor may not read were listed) — **done, A3a** (item 11).
   - The consent banner (`activeEncounter`, `MedicalRecordController::show`) — **done, A3a** (item 11).
   - The after-service workspace for `own` mode — **done, A3a** (item 11).
   - The book ANCHOR: `PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::resolveCanonicalWorkspaceVisit()` picks the earliest
     non-cancelled visit, which can be another doctor's pre-exam visit (a stale waiting visit, or a same-day visit
     with a lower id); with the flag ON `create` on it answered 404 and the workspace itself 404'd at that address.
     Changing the anchor changes the book selector (rule 237 §16, R7-1) for every role, so it was decided with A3 —
     **done, A3a** (item 11: the selector is unchanged; `startBook` is the `appendPage` rule; the anchor is never
     named nor its content shown).
   - The unified RM pages (`UnifiedMedicalRecordIndexRepository::nativeRecordsForPatient`, `latestNativeRecordsFor`)
     — **done, A3b** (item 11).
   - The prescription history (`RmePrescriptionService::showDataForVisit` → `historyForPatient`) — **done, A3b**
     (item 11).
   - The visit page's follow-up links (`rme/visits/show.blade.php`, "Kontrol dari" / "Kontrol lanjutan") and the
     odontogram page's parent link (`rme/visits/odontogram/show.blade.php`, "Odontogram Kunjungan Sebelumnya") —
     **done, A3b** (item 11).
   The A3 list is closed; the flag still stays OFF until A4 is merged and M3.3 is released (the author-stamp filter
   is in place, item 12).
   The prev/next visit arrows are NOT an A3 item: CC1-ADJ-1 (rule 227 §1.8c, its own PR, merged before
   this series) offers only a visit whose page the viewer may open, asked through that page's own ability, so the
   working-branch read scope applies now and the encounter scope when the flag is ON; it also corrects the
   prescription page's `'prev'` key.
9. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorPatientScopeEncounterWindowTest.php` (A1) and
   `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorPatientScopeSheetServiceHistoryTest.php` (A1b; A1c adds the workspace controls on a page
   of another doctor's sheet, flag ON and OFF) (critical-gate token `DoctorPatientScope`,
   declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`); the existing doctor-scope suites run unchanged with the flag OFF.
10. **Legacy archives (PR A2).** Revises rule 99 §1, §2, §11 (AIR-LRM-25/26) and rule 97 §5, §6 (AIR-LRM-27) for the
    flag ON; each carries a REVISED IN PART marker. With the flag ON, for a scoped Doctor:
    - **One answer.** `DoctorPatientScopeService::authorizeLegacyArchiveRead($user, $patient)` decides every read of a
      published legacy RME and legacy odontogram archive. An archive is patient-level history, so it is readable only
      in read mode `history` (`readModeFor()`, an own in-service encounter with the patient; never re-derived);
      `own` mode (the doctor's own old or cancelled visits) and assignment rows of every type grant nothing (plan
      default 1). Outside: **404** (`Response::denyAsNotFound`, plan default 6), so the archive's existence does not
      leak; an unlinked doctor account keeps its 403. Flag OFF: exactly `doctorCanAccessPatient()` as a plain bool
      (today's 403 through the policy).
    - **The doors.** `LegacyRmeRecordPolicy` and `LegacyOdontogramRecordPolicy` `view`/`viewFile` ask it (they now
      return `Response|bool`), so `rme.legacy-records.show|pages.show|source|print|export` and
      `rme.legacy-odontograms.show|pages.show|source` answer 404 outside the scope, after the branch-scoped
      `resolve()` (out-of-branch is still 404 first) and before the §1 download rule (no doctor-print audit row is
      written for a refused read). Inside the scope §1 is unchanged: page images 200, source/print/export 403 audited.
    - **Third layer.** Both controllers' `authorizeRead()` asks the policy and then
      `assertLegacyArchiveReadable($user, $patientId)` of the same authority, so a bypassed policy (`Gate::before`, a
      regression) still cannot serve a doctor; it is a no-op with the flag OFF (A1's pattern), so the flag-OFF read
      path is untouched even then. Super Admin is exempt by design (`shouldApplyDoctorScope()`), so nothing here
      must bind past `Gate::before`.
    - **Lists mirror the record.** `LegacyRmePatientHistoryService::publishedRecordsFor()` and
      `LegacyOdontogramPatientHistoryService::publishedRecordsFor()` ask the same method, so every list built on them
      shows an archive exactly when the record opens: the visit page timeline (`timelineFor`), the workspace clinical
      history, the inline legacy pages (`RmeWorkspacePageSequencer`), the legacy side of the document rail
      (`RmeWorkspaceDocumentPresenter`), the unified patient page (`UnifiedMedicalRecordIndexService::patientWorkspace`)
      and the native odontogram page's legacy list. The legacy lookups (`LegacyRmePatientLookupService`,
      `LegacyOdontogramPatientRepository`) were scoped in A1 (`applyPatientScopeForUser()`) and mirror it.
    - **Unchanged.** The archive branch gate (origin branch from the Nomor RM, `DoctorClinicalBranchResolver`,
      AIR-LRM-07/AIR-DAC-04), Front Office, Admin Klinik and the other non-doctor readers, the exempt roles, VOID
      handling and §1. **Not covered, recorded:** the staging import screens (`settings.rme.legacy-imports.*`,
      `settings.rme.legacy-odontograms.*`), the oversight legacy screens (`oversight.legacy.*`) and the archive void
      routes (their `void` policies apply no doctor scope; no seeded role holds the void permissions) never applied
      the doctor patient scope; a Doctor reaches them only through a second role's or a directly granted permission
      (intake, oversight, void), as with the native oversight screens in A1. Whether a dual-role Doctor is
      encounter-scoped there is an owner question (follow-up OD-MEMO-A2-1, alongside OD-MEMO-B-1).
    - **Existence (A2 review).** Every flag-ON scope refusal is `Response::denyAsNotFound()` WITHOUT a message
      (`readModeFor`-based legacy read, `outOfScope()`, `MedicalRecordPolicy`), so a JSON 404 out of scope is
      byte-identical to a 404 for an archive id that does not exist (both archive controllers' `resolve()` answer
      `abort(404, 'Not Found')`, the text `denyAsNotFound()` renders); the flag-OFF 403 keeps its message. `viewFile`
      decides on whether `view` allowed (`Response::allowed()`), never on `=== true`. Known limit, a precondition for
      ON: a native visit/sheet missing from route-model binding still answers JSON "No query results for model …",
      unlike the scope's "Not Found" (doctors use the Blade pages, whose 404 bodies are identical).
    - **Precondition for ON (A4).** The window of item 2 counts a stale open visit (an old `registered`, `waiting`,
      `in_progress` or `cashier_pending` visit) as in service, and A2 extends that reach to every legacy archive of
      the patient; the A4 reconcile dry run must report such visits before the flag is turned ON.
    - **Tests.** `tests/Feature/LegacyRme/LegacyRmeDoctorEncounterScopeTest.php` (token `LegacyRme`) and
      `tests/Feature/LegacyOdontogram/LegacyOdontogramDoctorEncounterScopeTest.php` (token `LegacyOdontogram`), both
      declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.
11. **The after-service workspace and its lists (PRs A3a, A3b).** With the flag ON, for a scoped Doctor
    (`DoctorPatientScopeService::encounterScopeAppliesTo()`; the exempt roles, other accounts and the flag OFF answer
    every point below exactly as before):
    - **The address.** Every visit's RM URL redirects to the patient's canonical anchor visit (rule 237 §16, R7-1;
      unchanged for every role and with the flag OFF). That address is where the book is stored, not the encounter
      being read, so `MedicalRecordController::show` asks `MedicalRecordPolicy::viewWorkspace` there (any other
      visit's RM URL still asks that visit's `view`): it opens the workspace by read mode
      (`DoctorPatientScopeService::authorizeWorkspaceRead()`): `history` → everything the per-record checks admit,
      `own` → the after-service workspace, `none` → 404; an unlinked account keeps its 403. Flag OFF / exempt:
      `viewWorkspace` is the anchor visit's own `view`. Deliberately, the rule 138 room scope is not asked of the
      anchor at this address (in the clinic another doctor's waiting anchor sits in that doctor's room, so asking it
      would keep the 404/403 this item removes); it keeps guarding the anchor's own pages (visit, odontogram,
      prescription, print). The working-branch scope is kept (review LOW-1): `viewWorkspace` also requires
      `RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()` on the anchor's branch, as the anchor's own `view` did, so a context-bound
      dual-role Doctor (also Perawat, Kasir or Admin Klinik) opens only an anchor in their working branch. Of an
      anchor the doctor may not read the workspace shows only the book's handwriting pages (below).
    - **Lists mirror the sheet read rule.** `PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::sheetsForPatient($patientId, $viewer)` and
      `orderedHandwritingBookForPatient($patientId, $viewer)` keep only
      `MedicalRecordSheetWriteScope::readableSheets()`, the list mirror of `MedicalRecordPolicy::view`'s doctor scope
      (a per-visit sheet by its own visit, `doctorCanAccessVisit()`; the book patient-level, `history`; the same
      `followsItsVisit()` definition), resolving the book once per patient per call — a bookless patient included
      (review LOW-2) — with the read mode memoised, so no query per sheet. The sheet navigation, the native side of the
      document rail (`RmeWorkspaceDocumentPresenter`, fed the same sheets) and the book page sequence therefore never
      list a sheet, or a page of a sheet, the doctor may not open; a `?sheet=` or `source_visit_id` naming one is
      ignored (the source visit through `listsVisitFor()`); the save redirect's focus page is computed in the saver's
      own sequence (`MedicalRecordHandwritingController`). Every list shown to a person passes the viewer; server
      facts (whether the patient has sheets, `createDraft()`'s check, `stampWorkspaceFields()`'s rank) never do.
    - **Own mode (A3 item 7, decided).** A doctor with own visits and no own in-service encounter opens the workspace
      read-only, limited to their own per-visit sheets and those sheets' pages, their own visits in the history
      (item 6) and no legacy archive (item 10). The BOOK is not listed at all, the doctor's own book pages included:
      a page row's `doctor_id` is its storage visit's doctor, not its writer, so the book has no reliable per-page
      author until M3.3 PR2's author stamp (rule 240 §4) — revisit then. Nothing is writable (every write needs an
      own in-service encounter, item 5). Without an own sheet the empty state names nobody and offers nothing.
    - **Consent banner.** The workspace and its empty state pass the patient's current examination only when
      `listsVisitFor()` admits it; otherwise the doctor reads "Belum ada pemeriksaan Anda yang berjalan", which is
      true whether or not another doctor is examining, so the banner reveals nothing.
    - **The book ANCHOR (decision).** The anchor stays the R7-1 canonical visit for everyone. With the flag ON a
      doctor inside an own in-service encounter starts and appends to the book there even when the anchor is another
      doctor's pre-exam visit: the book is patient-level and the anchor is its storage. Re-anchoring the doctor's
      book on their own encounter was rejected as not containable: it changes `pickCanonicalVisitId()` /
      `selfAnchoredBook()`, and a later sheet on the earlier visit would become self-anchored and take the book over
      (the second-book defect R7-1 closed). Keeping the 404 was rejected: the doctor could write no RM for their own
      consented encounter until someone cancels or examines another doctor's, possibly stale, visit. Mechanics:
      `MedicalRecordPolicy::startBook` is the `appendPage` rule for a scoped Doctor with the flag ON (`history`, 404
      otherwise) and exactly `create` on the anchor otherwise; `MedicalRecordController::store` (the first sheet),
      `MedicalRecordService::startBook()` (re-asserted on the locked anchor) and the empty state's "Buat Halaman RM
      Pertama" ask it. This reconciles item 8's former sentence "the doctor cannot start the book": the create door
      answered 404 (`create` on the anchor) while the first handwriting save (`MedicalRecordHandwritingController`,
      no book yet) asserted consent and then `appendPage` on the new draft, a patient-level rule; the two doors
      disagreed, and neither was reachable from the UI only because the workspace itself 404'd at the anchor. Both
      doors are now one rule. Unchanged: typed content on a book started there needs the anchor's own doctor
      (`assertTypedWriteOn()`, 403), and the RME authoring gate requires the live encounter to be the doctor's own.
    - **The anchor is never named, its content never shown (review HIGH-1).** A sheet whose own visit the viewer may
      not read can only be the book on such an anchor. Everything bound to that visit is withheld, decided on the
      server by `MedicalRecordPolicy::viewAnchorContent` (= `view`, plus, while the encounter scope applies,
      `listsVisitFor()` on the sheet's own visit; exactly `view` otherwise), which `MedicalRecordController::show`
      asks of the active sheet (`anchorContentReadable`): the visit (number and date in the header, doctor, initial
      treatment and note, the follow-up alert, its performed treatments; "Kembali ke Kunjungan" goes to the source
      visit) and everything typed onto the sheet (the "Data SOAP (Legacy)" card, the structured diagnoses and their
      rollout state `diagnosisEnforcement`, the Final/Draft badge and the finalisation messages, "Riwayat
      Pencatatan" with recorded/finalized by and when). Only the handwriting pages remain. The sheet tab and the rail
      entry carry no number or date (`unnamedSheetIds`, `RmeWorkspaceDocumentPresenter::documentsFor()`). The empty
      state names the anchor only to a viewer who may read it (`workspaceVisitNamed`). The workspace's "Kontrol dari"
      link follows `listsVisitFor()`. The book's PAGES stay patient-level: see the precondition in item 8.
    - **A roomless pre-exam anchor (review MEDIUM-1).** `visit.room` (`EnsureVisitRoomAssigned`) guards the
      {clinicVisit} of the workspace routes (show, store, handwriting store), which is the storage anchor; a roomless
      anchor of another doctor bounced the workspace to that visit's page and the first-page POST created nothing.
      While the encounter scope applies and the URL visit is the canonical anchor the doctor may not read, the gate
      asks instead the visit the doctor works from: the request's `source_visit_id` (same patient, RME branch,
      readable), else the patient's live encounter when it is the doctor's own, else nothing (writes still need the
      doctor's own consented in-progress encounter). A readable anchor (a new patient's first visit is the doctor's
      own encounter) and every other route stay gated as before; with the flag OFF the bounce is unchanged
      (pre-existing, not a regression).
    - **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorPatientScopePostServiceWorkspaceTest.php` (token `DoctorPatientScope`,
      declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`): `history`, `own` and `none` modes, another doctor's pre-exam
      visit, Supervisor RME, flag OFF parity per surface, the anchor start and append without naming it, both doors
      one rule, the focus page, and the query budget (identity, read mode and book lookups constant in the sheet
      count, with and without a book); after review: another doctor's in-progress anchor carrying SOAP, a diagnosis
      and a finalizer in the same and another room (withheld with the flag ON, unchanged with it OFF), a roomless
      registered anchor (flag ON and OFF), the room gate kept on the doctor's own roomless visit, and a
      context-bound dual-role Doctor held to the working branch.
    - **The list surfaces outside the workspace (PR A3b).** Each lists a record exactly when the per-record check
      of its visit admits it, through one of two mirrors of `doctorCanAccessVisit()`: in SQL, the visit scope's
      twin (`applyVisitScopeForUser()`, attached through `whereHas('clinicVisit')` or an `EXISTS` on the record's
      visit, so a soft-deleted visit is excluded as the per-record check excludes it, and a row limit counts only
      readable rows); per visit in PHP, `listsVisitFor()`. Each is attached only while the encounter scope applies
      (`encounterScopeAppliesTo()`), so with the flag OFF and for the exempt roles every query and list is exactly
      as before.
      - The unified RM pages: `UnifiedMedicalRecordIndexService::scopeFor()` sets
        `UnifiedMedicalRecordScope::$nativeVisitScope`, which `UnifiedMedicalRecordIndexRepository` applies to
        `latestNativeRecordsFor()` (each index row's newest record is the newest the doctor may read; one query
        for the page, never one per row), `nativeRecordsForPatient()` (the patient page) and the native leg
        `nativeExists()` behind eligibility, the per-row count, the sort key, the status/date filters and the
        search over visit numbers and doctor names, so none of them can count or match a sheet the pages would
        not list. Both pages are reached only in read mode `history` (the patient scope of item 6), so a record is
        listed when its visit is the doctor's own or past the pre-exam stage. The book is a record of its anchor
        visit there: on an anchor the doctor may not read it is not listed (never over-listed; it stays reachable in
        the workspace, where it is not named).
      - The prescription history beside the prescription form: `RmePrescriptionService::showDataForVisit()` passes
        the same SQL twin to `RmePrescriptionRepositoryInterface::historyForPatient()`, applied before its 20-row
        limit, so in `own` mode only the prescriptions of the doctor's own visits are listed and never one of
        another doctor's pre-exam visit. `patientHistory()` (no viewer) is unchanged.
      - The visit page's "Kontrol dari" / "Kontrol lanjutan" links: `ClinicVisitController::show` passes
        `followUpOf` / `followUpVisits` filtered by `listsVisitFor()`; the odontogram page's "Odontogram Kunjungan
        Sebelumnya" card (`OdontogramController::show`) appears only when the parent visit passes it.
      - **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorPatientScopeListSurfacesTest.php` (token `DoctorPatientScope`, declared
        in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`): `history` and `own` modes, another doctor's pre-exam visit,
        Supervisor RME, flag OFF parity per surface, and the index's query count constant in the patient count.
12. **On the surfaces of M3.3 (rule 240) and the PTM (rule 241) — integration of this series onto them.** With the
    flag ON, for a scoped Doctor (`encounterScopeAppliesTo()`; the exempt roles, other accounts and the flag OFF
    answer every point below exactly as before):
    - **The book's pages follow their author stamp** (closes item 8's precondition). The book is read
      patient-level, but every page written since rule 240 names the encounter that wrote it (§4.4,
      `authored_clinic_visit_id`). `MedicalRecordSheetWriteScope::readableBookPages()` (a list; the stamps in two
      queries through `MedicalRecordHandwritingRepositoryInterface::pageAuthors()`, the authoring visits in one) and
      `bookPageReadable()` (one row) keep a stamped page of the BOOK only when every encounter that wrote it is a
      visit the doctor may read (`listsVisitFor()`); an unstamped page (written before rule 240, its encounter never
      recorded) stays patient-level like the book; a per-visit sheet's pages follow their sheet. Applied to the
      workspace page sequence and the save redirect's focus (`orderedHandwritingBookForPatient($viewer)`), the page
      image routes (`rme.handwritings.image`, `rme.handwriting-pages.image` through
      `MedicalRecordPolicy::viewHandwritingPage` = `view` plus the page's author; 404 for a refused page, the
      out-of-scope 403 of rule 240 §8.10(a) unchanged), a version page's locked pages
      (`RmeAmendmentService::versionDetailFor()`) and the RME print/PDF of a visit whose sheet is the book
      (`sheetPagesFor()`). A correction page appended by an approved amendment carries the amended encounter as
      its author, so it shows exactly where its full label does (rule 240 §9.5).
    - **A sheet's content is read with `viewAnchorContent`.** `RmeRecordVersionService::readAbility()` names it for
      a sheet (`view` for a chart, which already follows its visit): the version history and version pages
      (`versionsFor()`, `version()`, `versionHistoryFor()`) and every amendment surface (`maySeeSubject()`,
      `mayView()` for the requester and the approver, `inApproverScope()`, `RmeAmendmentActionRequest::authorize()`).
      So a book anchored to a visit the doctor may not read answers 404 for its versions and amendments — to the
      doctor who requested the amendment too (a finalizer from the flag-OFF co-authoring era); a per-visit sheet
      already followed its visit through `view`. The workspace withholds the "Amandemen" block (`amendmentPanel`)
      with the rest of such an anchor's content. Revises rule 240 §5.7, §6.3, §6.7 and §7.7 for the flag ON. The
      correction-page labels needed nothing: they already follow `ClinicVisitPolicy::view` (rule 240 §9.5).
    - **The PTM banner** (rule 241 §3): `MedicalRecordController::ptmBannerFor()` describes only the encounter
      `encounterNamedFor()` admits, so neither the PTM state nor a countersign link of another doctor's encounter
      is given to the page; the "Belum ada pemeriksaan Anda yang berjalan" variant lives in
      `rme/visits/partials/ptm-authoring-banner.blade.php`.
    - **Order with rule 240 §1.8.** `MedicalRecordService::updateDraft()`, `MedicalRecordDiagnosisService` (record,
      make primary, remove) and `DiagnosisRolloutService::grantOverride()` call `assertWritable()` after
      `assertSheetAuthoringAllowed()` (the authoring gate, then the lock), so a final sheet still answers with its
      lock before a missing PTM; `finalize()` asks gate → `assertWritable()` → the sheet lock. No-ops with the flag
      OFF. The Doctor no longer holds `manage_rme_consents` (rule 241 §4), so `RmeVisitConsentPolicy::create` under
      the scope binds only an account that still captures signatures (a dual role).
    - **Follow-ups, not preconditions.** (a) Own mode still lists no book page: the page routes ask the book's
      `view`, which needs `history`; listing the doctor's own book pages there needs the routes to admit an
      own-authored page in own mode. (b) The cashier's clinical summary (`RmeInvoiceController`) and the oversight
      displays are not doctor surfaces; a dual-role Doctor there is OD-MEMO-A2-1's question.
    - **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/DoctorPatientScopeAmendmentSurfacesTest.php` (token `DoctorPatientScope`,
      declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`): the book's pages by author (sequence, page route, the writer and
      the exempt roles, version page, print), versions and amendments of a book on an unreadable anchor (requester
      404, cancel refused, the doctor of record and Supervisor RME kept, no "Amandemen" block), the PTM banner;
      flag OFF parity per surface.
